pastex (2.19.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * DEVELOPMENT.md states the project's rule: it is in development, it moves
    forward, and it does not keep two ways to do one thing. The three classes
    of state are written down: content (the snippet files in Drive) is never
    touched, presentation metadata may be dropped, caches are always
    disposable.
  * BREAKING: the per-item Drive labels pastex_order and pastex_collapsed are
    ignored completely. pastex_index on the root folder is the only source of
    order and collapse state. A library without a current index is shown in
    Drive's order with every folder open. Snippet files are untouched.
  * BREAKING: pastex_index now carries "v": 2. A label with another version is
    discarded and rebuilt, not decoded field by field. The configuration cache
    likewise stores version 2 and treats any other version as absent, which
    asks the user to pick the folder again instead of reading an unknown shape.
  * BREAKING: minimum_chrome_version is 141, and the capability branches for
    older browsers are gone: sidePanel.close, setPanelBehavior and the panel
    toggle are assumed to exist, so the launcher is always a toggle and no
    "toggle" flag travels with the capability.
  * BREAKING: pastex-google-drive.json and pastex-sites.json are recognised by
    their canonical names only. A renamed file is reported as missing. The
    service-account key keeps content detection, because Google downloads it
    under a project-specific name.
  * The project speaks one language: every document, script message and code
    comment is English. ARSITEKTUR.md became ARCHITECTURE.md, ANALISIS.md
    became ANALYSIS.md, cara-jalankan-get-linux-mint.md became
    running-on-linux-mint.md, and lokasi-collapse-urutan.md became
    where-order-and-collapse-live.md. The old names are gone, not aliased.
  * One build path: debian/rules and debian/source/format are deleted, because
    scripts/build-deb.sh packs the .deb with dpkg-deb directly and nothing ever
    ran debhelper. debian/control no longer Build-Depends on debhelper.
    tests/test_static.py now fails if that second path comes back.
  * The local cache now degrades in one stated order when storage.local is
    full: every stored library complete, then this library alone complete, then
    this library alone compacted, then session memory only. Before, the compacted
    copy was tried before dropping another account's snapshot, so a library on
    screen could lose its bodies while an off-screen library kept its place.
    Nothing a user notices changes while the snapshot fits.
  * APT metadata is now dated and expiring: dists/<suite>/Release carries
    Valid-Until 30 days after Date, so an archive that stops being updated is
    refused by apt instead of trusted indefinitely (apt reports "Release file
    ... is expired" and applies no update). scripts/build-apt-repository.sh
    derives SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH itself, from the last commit, exactly as
    build-deb.sh does: before that, the Release file was dated "now", so two
    builds of the same commit differed and checksums.txt could not be checked
    by rebuilding. Acquire-By-Hash is no longer restated, because this archive
    has no by-hash tree and the client default already matches.
  * The pool publishes exactly one version. scripts/build-apt-repository.sh
    refuses an input directory holding two versions instead of uploading the
    older one alongside, and the test suite checks that refusal.
  * debian/changelog passes lintian again. Every entry carried 00:00:00 on the
    same day, so the newest entry was not newer than the one below it
    (latest-changelog-entry-without-new-date), and many weekdays did not match
    their date (debian-changelog-has-wrong-weekday). All 78 entries now carry
    their real weekday and are strictly newest-first; 2.19.0 is stamped with the
    moment it was built. tests/test_static.py enforces both rules, because CI
    lints the package with --fail-on error.
  * New scripts/test-deploy.sh deploys a build for testing on a real computer:
    --local builds and installs with apt on the machine holding the checkout,
    --serve publishes a local APT archive and serves it so another machine on
    the network can install it, and --uninstall removes it again. Test builds
    are signed with one stable key in the checkout (debian/pastex-test-crx-key.pem,
    ignored by Git) so the test extension id does not change between builds, and
    the script states that it is not a release. The generated installer checks
    the download against SHA256SUMS, uses its own apt source for one run and
    deletes it afterwards. Both modes were run end to end, including the
    install, the verification of the browser registration, the rebuild after a
    root build, and the removal.
  * New scripts/reset-pastex-state.sh clears the extension's local storage and
    says so: Drive is never touched.
  * DEVELOPMENT.md gains rule 6: one language, English. tests/test_language.py
    now scans every tracked file, tests and fixtures included, for Indonesian
    words (the last one was the sample text "baris pertama\n\nbaris kedua" in
    a content test, now "first line\n\nsecond line"), fails on a letter outside
    English and the UI glyph set, and fails when a renamed document is left
    behind as a copy or a document link points at a file that no longer exists.
  * New tests/test_development_policy.py fails when shipped code keeps a
    compatibility path, when a persisted shape loses its version, or when the
    policy document stops describing the three classes.

 -- Patrick Buhagiar <patrickbuhagiar24@users.noreply.github.com>  Mon, 23 Nov 2026 18:59:48 +0000

pastex (2.18.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Rename the APT archive suite from "mint" to "stable". The suite is the
    directory APT reads (dists/<suite>/), and "mint" read like a Linux Mint
    release codename, which it never was: this archive is one fixed label for
    an architecture-independent package.
  * The old dists/mint/ is no longer written. scripts/build-apt-repository.sh,
    scripts/sign-apt-repository.sh and scripts/setup-apt-repo.sh default to
    "stable"; packaging/pastex.sources and pastex-unsigned.sources ship
    Suites: stable; the Pages index documents dists/stable/; and the CI
    variable APT_SUITE is "stable".
  * Machines configured for "mint" will not see updates until the installed
    source file is updated. apt reports exactly one symptom:
        E: The repository '…/paste mint Release' does not have a Release file.
    Fix, with no change to the signing key:
        sudo sed -i 's/^Suites: mint$/Suites: stable/' \
          /etc/apt/sources.list.d/pastex.sources
        sudo apt update
    README.md and apt-repo/README.md both document this, including the check
    that tells the user whether migration is needed.
  * Tests follow the rename, so the suite name cannot silently disagree
    between the source file, the Release metadata and the archive path.

